	"butrintTitle": "Butrint",
	"butrintRegionTitle": "Sarandë, Albania",
	"butrintArtifactCulture": "Illyrian-Roman",
	"butrintPullQuote1Top": "A Microcosm of",
	"butrintPullQuote1Bottom": "Mediterranean History",
	"butrintPullQuote2": "Butrint is a testament to the richness and diversity of a cultural landscape shaped by centuries of human settlement.",
	"butrintPullQuote2Author": "UNESCO",
	"butrintCallout1": "Butrint has been inhabited since at least the 8th century BCE and served as a Greek colony, a Roman city, a Byzantine bishopric, and a Venetian outpost.",
	"butrintCallout2": "The ancient theater, built in the 3rd century BCE, could seat approximately 1,500 spectators and is one of the best-preserved in the region.",
	"butrintVideoCaption": "“Butrint Albania | The ruins of Butrinti Albania.” Visit Albania, YouTube.",
	"butrintMapCaption": "Map showing location of Butrint National Park in southern Albania.",
	"butrintHistoryInfo1": "Butrint is an archaeological site in southern Albania, opposite the Greek island of Corfu. First settled by Greek colonists from Corfu in the 8th century BCE, it grew into an important city of the ancient world. According to legend, the city was founded by Trojan refugees led by Aeneas.\nButrint became a Roman protectorate in 228 BCE, and Julius Caesar later designated it a colony for veterans. Under Roman rule, the city flourished with the construction of an aqueduct, public baths, and a forum.",
	"butrintHistoryInfo2": "Butrint continued to thrive as an important ecclesiastical center in the Byzantine period. A large basilica and baptistery were built in the 6th century CE. The site changed hands many times \u2014 from Byzantines to Angevins, from Venetians to Ottomans \u2014 each leaving their architectural mark on this remarkable landscape.",
	"butrintConstructionInfo1": "The ancient theater is carved into the southern slope of the acropolis hill, taking advantage of the natural terrain. Built with local limestone in the Hellenistic period, the theater features a semicircular orchestra, tiered seating (cavea), and a stage building (skene).\nThe nearby Asclepion sanctuary, dedicated to the god of healing, features finely carved stone reliefs and columns in the Corinthian style.",
	"butrintConstructionInfo2": "The 6th-century baptistery is one of the largest early Christian structures in the Balkans. Its floor is adorned with intricate mosaics depicting animals and geometric patterns, showcasing the artistic sophistication of the era. The structure uses a circular plan with 16 granite columns supporting the interior.",
	"butrintLocationInfo1": "Butrint is located on a peninsula between the Vivari Channel and Lake Butrint in the Ksamil area of southern Albania, approximately 14 miles (23 km) south of Sarand\u00eb. The site sits within a national park encompassing wetlands, forests, and the lake itself.",
	"butrintLocationInfo2": "The strategic position of Butrint, on a narrow strait between the mainland and Corfu, made it an important stop along ancient Mediterranean trade routes. The surrounding landscape of marshes, lagoons, and the Ionian Sea coast has been crucial to the site's history as both a defensive position and a thriving port.",
	"butrint228bce": "Butrint becomes a Roman protectorate, together with Corfu.",
	"butrint44bce": "Julius Caesar makes Butrint a colony for his veterans; under Augustus the town doubles in size.",
	"butrint1386ce": "Venice buys Corfu and Butrint from the Angevins and keeps Butrint as an outpost.",
	"butrint1798ce": "Ali Pasha of Tepelena takes Butrint from the French.",
	"butrint1928ce": "Italian archaeologist Luigi Maria Ugolini begins the first modern excavations.",
	"butrint1992ce": "Butrint is inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List.",

	"beratTitle": "Berat",
	"beratRegionTitle": "Berat, Albania",
	"beratArtifactCulture": "Byzantine-Albanian",
	"beratPullQuote1Top": "A Rare Example of",
	"beratPullQuote1Bottom": "Well-Preserved Ottoman Architecture",
	"beratPullQuote2": "Berat bears witness to the coexistence of various religious and cultural communities down the centuries.",
	"beratPullQuote2Author": "UNESCO",
	"beratCallout1": "Berat is known for its distinctive white Ottoman houses climbing up the hillside, their many windows giving the city its famous epithet.",
	"beratCallout2": "The castle quarter (Kalaja) has been continuously inhabited for over 2,400 years and still houses a living community within its 13th-century walls.",
	"beratVideoCaption": "“Berat, Albania - The pride of Albanian architecture.” Visit Albania, YouTube.",
	"beratMapCaption": "Map showing location of Berat in central Albania.",
	"beratHistoryInfo1": "Berat is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Albania, located along the banks of the Osum River. The castle hill has been inhabited since at least the 7th century BCE; in antiquity the fortified town here was known as Antipatrea, a name probably honouring the Macedonian regent Antipater.\nUnder Roman rule, the city served as a strategic military outpost along the Via Egnatia trade route.",
	"beratHistoryInfo2": "During the Byzantine period, the city was known as Pulcheriopolis and later Beligrad. The Ottomans captured Berat in 1417 and it became a major center of trade and craftsmanship.\nThe city's three historic quarters \u2014 Mangalem, Gorica, and Kalaja \u2014 preserve an extraordinary blend of Eastern and Western architecture, earning it UNESCO World Heritage status in 2008 as part of the \"Historic Centres of Berat and Gjirokast\u00ebr.\"",
	"beratConstructionInfo1": "The Berat Castle sits at 614 feet (187 m) above sea level atop a rocky hill. The current walls date primarily to the 13th century under the Despotate of Epirus, though foundations are much older. The fortress encompasses an area of about 24 acres (10 hectares) and originally had 24 towers along its walls.\nWithin the castle walls, several Byzantine churches survive, including the Church of the Holy Trinity and the Church of St. Mary of Blachernae, featuring remarkable 16th-century frescoes by the renowned painter Onufri.",
	"beratConstructionInfo2": "The Mangalem quarter below the castle is famous for its Ottoman-era houses built on the steep hillside. These traditional houses feature stone foundations, timber-framed upper stories, and large windows arranged symmetrically on whitewashed facades \u2014 the architectural signature that gives Berat its name \"City of a Thousand Windows.\"",
	"beratLocationInfo1": "Berat is situated in south-central Albania, approximately 75 miles (120 km) south of the capital Tirana. The city is built along both banks of the Osum River, at the foot of Mount Tomorr, one of Albania's most sacred mountains reaching 8,042 feet (2,416 m).",
	"beratLocationInfo2": "The Osum River canyon, stretching southeast of the city, is one of the most dramatic natural features in Albania, with vertical canyon walls reaching over 260 feet (80 m). The surrounding region has been cultivated since antiquity, producing olives, grapes, and figs in the mild Mediterranean-influenced climate.",
	"berat200bce": "The Romans storm and burn Antipatrea, the fortified town on Berat's hill.",
	"berat1417ce": "Berat becomes part of the Ottoman Empire.",
	"berat1547ce": "The icon painter Onufri signs a church fresco dated 23 July 1547; Berat's castle museum now bears his name.",
	"berat1922ce": "The Congress of Berat proclaims the autocephaly of the Albanian Orthodox Church.",
	"berat1961ce": "Berat is declared a \"Museum City\", protecting its historic quarters.",
	"berat2008ce": "Berat joins Gjirokastër on the UNESCO World Heritage List.",

	"gjirokasterTitle": "Gjirokast\u00ebr",
	"gjirokasterRegionTitle": "Gjirokast\u00ebr, Albania",
	"gjirokasterArtifactCulture": "Ottoman-Albanian",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote1Top": "A Rare Example of",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote1Bottom": "a Well-Preserved Ottoman Town",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote2": "Gjirokast\u00ebr is an outstanding example of a well-preserved Ottoman town, built by farmers of large estates.",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote2Author": "UNESCO",
	"gjirokasterCallout1": "The Gjirokast\u00ebr Fortress is one of the largest in the Balkans, dominating the city from a hilltop at over 1,100 feet (336 m) above sea level.",
	"gjirokasterCallout2": "The city's distinctive tower houses, known as \"kull\u00eb,\" feature slate-stone roofs and fortified architecture unique to the region, giving Gjirokast\u00ebr its name \"The Stone City.\"",
	"gjirokasterVideoCaption": "“Gjirokaster Albania | Gjirokastra Albania.” Visit Albania, YouTube.",
	"gjirokasterMapCaption": "Map showing location of Gjirokast\u00ebr in southern Albania.",
	"gjirokasterHistoryInfo1": "Gjirokastër is a city in southern Albania, situated in the Drino River valley. The first fortress on the hilltop dates to the 5th–6th centuries CE. The name Gjirokastër likely derives from the medieval Greek \"Argyrokastron\" meaning \"Silver Castle.\"\nThe city rose to prominence in the 13th century as a center of the Despotate of Epirus and later became an important stronghold contested between Byzantine, Serbian, and Ottoman forces.",
	"gjirokasterHistoryInfo2": "Under Ottoman rule from 1418 onward, Gjirokastër became the center of a large sanjak (administrative district) and thrived as a bazaar city. Wealthy landowners built the distinctive fortified tower houses that define the city's character.\nThe city was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005 as \"a rare example of a well-preserved Ottoman town, built by farmers of large estates.\" It is the birthplace of both former Albanian dictator Enver Hoxha and Nobel Prize-winning author Ismail Kadare.",
	"gjirokasterConstructionInfo1": "The Gjirokast\u00ebr Fortress covers an area of approximately 18 acres (7.3 hectares) on the hilltop. Expanded significantly by Ali Pasha of Ioannina in the early 19th century, the fortress features massive stone walls, a clock tower, an aqueduct, and a series of underground tunnels and chambers.\nDuring the communist era, the fortress was used as a prison and later converted into a Museum of Armaments. Today it hosts the Gjirokast\u00ebr National Folklore Festival every five years.",
	"gjirokasterConstructionInfo2": "The city's iconic tower houses (kull\u00eb) are typically three to four stories tall, built of solid stone with slate roofs. The ground floor served as a cistern and storage, the first floor for winter living, and the upper floors for summer quarters and guest reception. Defensive features include small windows on lower floors and fortified entrances.",
	"gjirokasterLocationInfo1": "Gjirokast\u00ebr is located in the Drino River valley in southern Albania, about 18 miles (29 km) from the Greek border. The city is built on the steep slopes of the Mali i Gjer\u00eb mountain range, rising from the valley floor at approximately 980 feet (300 m) to the castle at over 1,100 feet (336 m).",
	"gjirokasterLocationInfo2": "The surrounding region, known as Dropull, has been a crossroads of cultures for millennia. The Drino Valley connects the Adriatic coast with the interior Balkans, and the ancient route from the coast at Butrint passed through this valley toward the Via Egnatia.",
	"gjirokaster1336ce": "Byzantine emperor John VI Kantakouzenos records the town as Argyrokastro, the first mention of its name.",
	"gjirokaster1418ce": "After a siege, the Ottomans take the city from the Zenebishi family.",
	"gjirokaster1811ce": "Gjirokastër becomes part of Ali Pasha's Pashalik of Yanina; he later enlarges the castle.",
	"gjirokaster1932ce": "King Zog's government expands the prison inside the castle.",
	"gjirokaster1936ce": "Writer Ismail Kadare is born in the city.",
	"gjirokaster2005ce": "The historic centre is inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List.",

	"apolloniaTitle": "Apollonia",
	"apolloniaRegionTitle": "Fier, Albania",
	"apolloniaArtifactCulture": "Illyrian-Greek",
	"apolloniaPullQuote1Top": "An Admirable City",
	"apolloniaPullQuote1Bottom": "With Excellent Laws",
	"apolloniaPullQuote2": "Apollonia is considered one of the most important cities of the ancient world, a center of learning where the young Octavian studied.",
	"apolloniaPullQuote2Author": "Strabo",
	"apolloniaCallout1": "The Roman statesman and orator Cicero described Apollonia as \"a great and important city\" (magna urbs et gravis) in his work Philippics.",
	"apolloniaCallout2": "The young Octavian (later Emperor Augustus) was studying in Apollonia when he received news of Julius Caesar's assassination in 44 BCE, a pivotal moment in Roman history.",
	"apolloniaVideoCaption": "“Apollonia, Albania by Drone.” Visit Albania, YouTube.",
	"apolloniaMapCaption": "Map showing location of Apollonia near Fier in central Albania.",
	"apolloniaHistoryInfo1": "Apollonia was an ancient Greek colony founded in 588 BCE by settlers from Corinth and Corcyra (modern Corfu) on the banks of the Aous River (modern Vjosa) in Illyria. Named after the god Apollo, the city quickly grew into one of the most important settlements on the eastern Adriatic coast.\nAt its peak, Apollonia had a population of around 60,000 and was a major stop along the Via Egnatia, the great Roman road connecting the Adriatic to Constantinople.",
	"apolloniaHistoryInfo2": "The city was renowned as a center of philosophy and learning. Numerous Roman nobles sent their sons to study there, most notably Octavian, the future Emperor Augustus. Apollonia's importance declined after the 3rd century CE when an earthquake altered the course of the Vjosa River, destroying the city's harbor and cutting off its trade routes.\nThe site was partially built over by the Byzantine-era Monastery of St. Mary, which still stands and houses an archaeological museum.",
	"apolloniaConstructionInfo1": "The archaeological park covers approximately 237 acres (96 hectares), though only a fraction has been excavated. Major structures include a monumental bouleuterion (council house) with 19 rows of stone seating, an impressive stoa (covered walkway) measuring over 260 feet (80 m) in length, and a well-preserved odeon (small theater).\nThe remains of a nymphaeum (ornamental fountain) and several temples demonstrate the city's wealth and architectural sophistication.",
	"apolloniaConstructionInfo2": "The city was built using locally quarried limestone and followed classical Greek urban planning with an agora, temples, and public buildings arranged along paved streets. The defensive walls, encircling approximately 1 square mile (2.5 sq km), were built with polygonal masonry typical of Hellenistic military architecture.\nThe 13th-century Monastery of St. Mary, built atop ancient ruins within the archaeological zone, features a beautifully decorated Byzantine church with carved stone facades.",
	"apolloniaLocationInfo1": "Apollonia is situated on a hilltop near the village of Pojani, approximately 7 miles (12 km) west of the city of Fier in central Albania. The site overlooks the Myzeqe plain and the Vjosa River valley, commanding views extending to the Adriatic Sea.",
	"apolloniaLocationInfo2": "In antiquity, the Vjosa River flowed much closer to the city, providing direct access to the Adriatic coast and enabling Apollonia to function as an important port city. The fertile surrounding plain supported agricultural production, while the hilltop position offered natural defensive advantages.",
	"apollonia588bce": "Greek colonists from Corinth and Corcyra found Apollonia on Illyrian land.",
	"apollonia229bce": "Apollonia comes under the control of the Roman Republic.",
	"apollonia148bce": "Apollonia becomes part of the new Roman province of Macedonia.",
	"apollonia44bce": "Young Octavian, the future Emperor Augustus, is studying in Apollonia.",
	"apollonia1272ce": "The Byzantine Monastery of St. Mary is built within the ruins of the ancient city.",
	"apollonia1924ce": "A French archaeological mission begins systematic excavations, which last until 1938.",

	"thethTitle": "Thethi",
	"thethRegionTitle": "Shkod\u00ebr, Albania",
	"thethArtifactCulture": "Albanian Highland",
	"thethPullQuote1Top": "A Living Fragment of",
	"thethPullQuote1Bottom": "Europe's Last Wilderness",
	"thethPullQuote1Author": "Edith Durham",
	"thethPullQuote2": "The Albanian Alps are perhaps the last great wilderness in Europe, a place where time seems to have stood still.",
	"thethPullQuote2Author": "Edith Durham",
	"thethCallout1": "Thethi's iconic Lock-in Tower (Kulla e Ngujimit) was used as a refuge for men involved in blood feuds under the Kanun of L\u00ebk\u00eb Dukagjini, the traditional Albanian code of law.",
	"thethCallout2": "The Thethi valley sits at approximately 2,500 feet (760 m) above sea level, surrounded by peaks exceeding 8,000 feet (2,500 m), including the dramatic Radohima massif.",
	"thethVideoCaption": "“Theth Albania - Albanian Alps.” Visit Albania, YouTube.",
	"thethMapCaption": "Map showing location of Thethi in the Albanian Alps, northern Albania.",
	"thethHistoryInfo1": "Thethi is a remote alpine village nestled in a glacial valley in the Albanian Alps (Bjeshk\u00ebt e Nemuna), in the Shkod\u00ebr district of northern Albania. The valley has been inhabited for centuries by Albanian highland clans, who maintained a fiercely independent way of life governed by the Kanun, a set of customary laws attributed to L\u00ebk\u00eb Dukagjini.\nThe isolation of the valley \u2014 accessible only by mountain passes for most of its history \u2014 preserved a traditional way of life largely unchanged until the 20th century.",
	"thethHistoryInfo2": "During the communist period (1944-1991), the Albanian government attempted to modernize the highlands, building roads and schools but also restricting traditional customs. After the fall of communism, many highland villages were depopulated as residents moved to lowland cities.\nIn recent years, Thethi has experienced a revival as a destination for hiking and ecotourism, with the Peaks of the Balkans trail bringing international attention to the region's natural beauty and cultural heritage.",
	"thethConstructionInfo1": "Thethi's traditional stone houses (sht\u00ebpi) are built from local limestone and timber, designed to withstand harsh mountain winters. The houses typically feature thick stone walls, small windows to conserve heat, and wooden balconies. Stone-slab roofs, anchored with heavy rocks, protect against the heavy snowfall that blankets the valley for several months each year.\nThe most famous structure in Thethi is the Lock-in Tower (Kulla e Ngujimit), a stone tower used historically as a place of refuge for men targeted in blood feuds.",
	"thethConstructionInfo2": "The Church of Thethi (Kisha e Thethit), built in the 19th century, is a small but picturesque stone church set against the dramatic mountain backdrop. Traditional highland architecture throughout the valley includes watermills along the Thethi River, stone bridges, and livestock enclosures built with dry-stone walling techniques passed down through generations.",
	"thethLocationInfo1": "Thethi lies within the Thethi National Park, established in 1966, covering approximately 5,200 acres (2,100 hectares) of pristine alpine wilderness. The valley is surrounded by the peaks of the Albanian Alps, including Radohima at 8,146 feet (2,568 m) and Haj-Nehaj at 8,524 feet (2,598 m).",
	"thethLocationInfo2": "The Blue Eye of Thethi (Syri i Kalt\u00ebr), a stunning natural spring pool of vivid turquoise water, is one of the valley's most remarkable natural features. The Grunas Waterfall, cascading 100 feet (30 m) down a limestone cliff, is another iconic landmark. The valley connects to Valbona via a popular mountain pass trek at approximately 5,900 feet (1,800 m).",
	"theth1892ce": "The village church of Theth is built.",
	"theth1908ce": "Edith Durham travels through the highlands and describes Theth in High Albania (1909).",
	"theth1966ce": "Theth National Park is created.",
	"theth2011ce": "The Peaks of the Balkans trail opens, linking Theth with Kosovo and Montenegro.",
	"theth2017ce": "Albanologist Robert Elsie is laid to rest in Theth.",
	"theth2022ce": "Theth becomes part of the new Alps of Albania National Park.",

	"krujaTitle": "Kruj\u00eb",
	"krujaRegionTitle": "Durr\u00ebs County, Albania",
	"krujaArtifactCulture": "Medieval Albanian",
	"krujaPullQuote1Top": "The Shield of Christendom",
	"krujaPullQuote1Bottom": "Against the Ottoman Tide",
	"krujaPullQuote1Author": "Pope Callixtus III",
	"krujaPullQuote2": "Skanderbeg's defense of Kruj\u00eb is one of the most remarkable episodes of resistance in European military history.",
	"krujaPullQuote2Author": "Fan Noli",
	"krujaCallout1": "Gjergj Kastrioti Skanderbeg successfully defended Kruj\u00eb against three major Ottoman sieges, earning him the title \"Athleta Christi\" (Champion of Christ) from the Pope.",
	"krujaCallout2": "The castle sits at an elevation of approximately 1,970 feet (600 m) atop the Skanderbeg Mountain (Mali i Sariut), providing a commanding view of the surrounding plains.",
	"krujaVideoCaption": "“KRUJA CITY & CASTLE 2021 | 4K DRONE CINEMATIC VIDEO.” GOGLA, YouTube.",
	"krujaMapCaption": "Map showing location of Kruj\u00eb in central Albania.",
	"krujaHistoryInfo1": "Kruj\u00eb is a city in north-central Albania, historically renowned as the seat of Gjergj Kastrioti Skanderbeg (1405-1468), Albania's national hero. The castle of Kruj\u00eb has been fortified since at least the 5th century and became the capital of the Principality of Kastrioti in the 15th century.\nIn 1443, Skanderbeg raised the Albanian flag over the castle after abandoning his forced service in the Ottoman army, beginning a 25-year campaign of resistance against the Ottoman Empire.",
	"krujaHistoryInfo2": "Skanderbeg successfully defended Kruj\u00eb against three major Ottoman sieges \u2014 in 1450, 1466, and 1467 \u2014 each time repelling vastly superior forces. His resistance prevented Ottoman expansion into Western Europe for decades and earned him admiration across the Christian world.\nAfter Skanderbeg's death in 1468, the castle fell to the Ottomans in 1478 after a prolonged fourth siege. The city remained under Ottoman control until Albanian independence in 1912.",
	"krujaConstructionInfo1": "The Kruj\u00eb Castle is perched on a rocky outcrop at the edge of Skanderbeg Mountain (Mali i Sariut). The fortress walls follow the natural contours of the terrain, encircling an area that once contained a small town with houses, churches, and a bazaar.\nThe Skanderbeg Museum, designed by architects Pranvera Hoxha and Pirro Vaso and opened in 1982, sits within the castle walls. Built in a modernist style inspired by medieval Albanian architecture, it houses artifacts from the period of Albanian resistance.",
	"krujaConstructionInfo2": "Below the castle, the restored Old Bazaar (Pazari i Vjet\u00ebr) stretches along a cobblestone street with traditional stone and timber shops. The bazaar has been in continuous use since the 15th century and today sells traditional Albanian crafts, antiques, and locally made goods. The Dollma Tekke, a small Bektashi shrine within the castle complex, features elegant Ottoman-era architecture with painted interiors.",
	"krujaLocationInfo1": "Kruj\u00eb is located approximately 20 miles (32 km) north of Tirana in central Albania. The castle sits atop a mountain spur overlooking the vast western Albanian plain that stretches to the Adriatic Sea, providing strategic visibility over approaching forces \u2014 a key factor in Skanderbeg's successful defense.",
	"krujaLocationInfo2": "The Skanderbeg Mountain (Mali i Sariut) rises steeply behind the castle to approximately 3,500 feet (1,070 m). The surrounding terrain of steep gorges and dense forests provided natural defensive advantages that Skanderbeg exploited in his guerrilla tactics against the much larger Ottoman forces. The nearby Sarisalltiku peak features a cave shrine venerated by both Muslims and Christians.",
	"kruja1190ce": "Krujë becomes the capital of the Principality of Arbër, the first Albanian state of the Middle Ages.",
	"kruja1443ce": "On 28 November, Skanderbeg takes Krujë and raises the Albanian flag over the castle.",
	"kruja1450ce": "Skanderbeg's forces withstand the first Ottoman siege, led by Sultan Murad II.",
	"kruja1478ce": "After a siege of more than a year, Krujë falls to the Ottomans.",
	"kruja1982ce": "The Skanderbeg Museum opens beside the castle.",
	"kruja1989ce": "The National Ethnographic Museum opens in a Toptani family house built in 1764.",

	"albanianAlpsTitle": "Albanian Alps",
	"albanianAlpsRegionTitle": "Northern Albania",
	"albanianAlpsArtifactCulture": "Albanian Highland",
	"albanianAlpsPullQuote1Top": "Europe's Last Great",
	"albanianAlpsPullQuote1Bottom": "Untouched Wilderness",
	"albanianAlpsPullQuote2": "The Accursed Mountains stand as a testament to the raw, untamed beauty of the natural world.",
	"albanianAlpsPullQuote2Author": "Edith Durham",
	"albanianAlpsCallout1": "The Albanian Alps, known locally as Bjeshk\u00ebt e Nemuna (Accursed Mountains), contain some of the most pristine ecosystems in Europe, harboring lynx, wolves, bears, and golden eagles.",
	"albanianAlpsCallout2": "Jezerca, the highest peak at 8,839 feet (2,694 m), is one of the most dramatic limestone summits in the Balkans, featuring permanent snowfields and glacial cirques.",
	"albanianAlpsVideoCaption": "“Valbona, Albania.” Visit Albania, YouTube.",
	"albanianAlpsMapCaption": "Map showing location of the Albanian Alps in northern Albania.",
	"albanianAlpsHistoryInfo1": "The Albanian Alps (Bjeshk\u00ebt e Nemuna), also known as the Accursed Mountains or the Prokletije, are a mountain range stretching across northern Albania, western Kosovo, and eastern Montenegro. In Albania, the range includes some of the most remote and pristine wilderness in all of Europe.\nThe mountains have been home to Albanian highland clans for centuries, who developed a unique culture of hospitality, honor, and self-governance codified in the Kanun of L\u00ebk\u00eb Dukagjini.",
	"albanianAlpsHistoryInfo2": "The name \"Accursed Mountains\" has various folk etymologies \u2014 some say it comes from the difficulty of crossing them, others attribute it to a legend of feuding mountains cursed by the gods. The Albanian name \"Bjeshk\u00ebt e Nemuna\" translates more accurately as \"Mountains of the Highlanders.\"\nBritish traveler Edith Durham extensively documented the highlands in the early 1900s, describing a society where ancient customs of blood feuds and sworn virgins (burrnesha) persisted alongside extraordinary hospitality and natural beauty.",
	"albanianAlpsConstructionInfo1": "Unlike man-made structures, the Albanian Alps are a monument of nature, sculpted by millions of years of geological processes. The range is composed primarily of Mesozoic-era limestone and dolomite, shaped by glacial and karst erosion into dramatic peaks, deep valleys, and underground cave systems.\nThe Blue Eye of Theth, turquoise glacial pools, and the Valbona valley's dramatic cliff faces are all products of this ongoing geological story.",
	"albanianAlpsConstructionInfo2": "Human structures in the Albanian Alps reflect the mountain environment. Traditional highland houses (kullë) are built from massive limestone blocks with timber interiors, designed to withstand heavy snowfall and provide defense. Stone-walled pastures, ancient mule trails, and small mountain churches dot the landscape.\nThe Peaks of the Balkans trail, a 120-mile (192 km) hiking route opened in 2011, connects communities across three countries using many of these traditional paths.",
	"albanianAlpsLocationInfo1": "The Albanian Alps extend across the districts of Shkod\u00ebr and Kuk\u00ebs in northern Albania, forming the country's border with Montenegro and Kosovo. The range stretches approximately 40 miles (65 km) from the Kelmendi region in the northwest to the Valbona area in the east. Major valleys include Thethi, Valbona, and Kelmendi.",
	"albanianAlpsLocationInfo2": "The Valbona Valley National Park and Thethi National Park protect much of the Albanian portion of the range. Lake Koman, accessible by ferry through a dramatic canyon often compared to Scandinavian fjords, provides a stunning approach to the mountains from the south. The region receives some of the highest precipitation in Europe, feeding numerous rivers and waterfalls.",
	"albanianAlps1909ce": "Edith Durham publishes High Albania, the classic account of life among the highland clans.",
	"albanianAlps1929ce": "British climbers make the first recorded ascent of Maja Jezercë (2,694 m), the range's highest peak.",
	"albanianAlps1996ce": "Valbona Valley National Park is created.",
	"albanianAlps2009ce": "A glacier is discovered in the Albanian part of the range.",
	"albanianAlps2011ce": "The Peaks of the Balkans trail opens across Albania, Kosovo and Montenegro.",
	"albanianAlps2022ce": "Theth, Valbona and nearby reserves merge into the Alps of Albania National Park.",

	"korcaTitle": "Kor\u00e7\u00eb",
	"korcaRegionTitle": "Kor\u00e7\u00eb, Albania",
	"korcaArtifactCulture": "Albanian Orthodox",
	"korcaPullQuote1Top": "Not Just a Cathedral,",
	"korcaPullQuote1Bottom": "but the Soul of a Nation",
	"korcaPullQuote1Author": "Fan Noli",
	"korcaPullQuote2": "Kor\u00e7\u00eb is the city where Albanian national consciousness first found its voice through schools, literature, and art.",
	"korcaPullQuote2Author": "Faik Konica",
	"korcaCallout1": "The Resurrection Cathedral (Katedralja Ngjallja e Krishtit) is the largest Orthodox cathedral in Albania, designed by renowned architect Fan Noli and completed in 2003.",
	"korcaCallout2": "Kor\u00e7\u00eb is home to the first Albanian-language school, opened on March 7, 1887, a date now celebrated as Teacher's Day (Dita e M\u00ebsuesit) across Albania.",
	"korcaVideoCaption": "“Korçë, Dardhë | Dardhe Korce Albania.” Visit Albania, YouTube.",
	"korcaMapCaption": "Map showing location of Kor\u00e7\u00eb in southeastern Albania.",
	"korcaHistoryInfo1": "Kor\u00e7\u00eb is a city in southeastern Albania, situated in a high plateau at approximately 2,800 feet (850 m) above sea level. The city has been a center of Albanian culture, education, and art since the 19th century, earning it the title \"the cradle of Albanian culture.\"\nThe first Albanian-language school was opened here on March 7, 1887, by Pandeli Sotiri, marking a pivotal moment in the Albanian national awakening. Kor\u00e7\u00eb also hosted the first Albanian printing press and numerous literary societies.",
	"korcaHistoryInfo2": "The original Orthodox Cathedral was destroyed during the communist period when Albania declared itself the world's first atheist state in 1967, demolishing nearly all religious buildings. The new Resurrection Cathedral (Katedralja Ngjallja e Krishtit) was built between 1994 and 2002 on the foundations of the old cathedral, symbolizing both religious revival and national cultural renewal.\nKorçë is also renowned for its contribution to Albanian music, art, and the famous annual Korçë Beer Festival, one of the largest in the Balkans.",
	"korcaConstructionInfo1": "The Resurrection Cathedral was designed in a neo-Byzantine style, drawing inspiration from traditional Orthodox church architecture while incorporating modern construction techniques. The cathedral features a cruciform plan with a large central dome reaching approximately 100 feet (30 m) in height, flanked by smaller domes.\nThe exterior is clad in light-colored stone with decorative arched windows, while the interior is adorned with frescoes and iconography painted by Albanian and Greek artists.",
	"korcaConstructionInfo2": "The cathedral was built between 1994 and 2002. Modern reinforced concrete and steel frame construction was used for structural integrity, while traditional stone and decorative elements maintain the Byzantine aesthetic.\nThe bell tower, rising beside the cathedral, houses a set of bells that ring across the city. The surrounding plaza and park create a cultural center for the city, connecting to the National Museum of Medieval Art, which houses one of the finest collections of Byzantine icons in the world.",
	"korcaLocationInfo1": "Kor\u00e7\u00eb is located in the Kor\u00e7\u00eb Basin, a fertile plateau surrounded by mountains in southeastern Albania. The city lies approximately 112 miles (180 km) southeast of Tirana, near the borders with both Greece and North Macedonia. The Morava Mountain range rises to the east, while Lake Ohrid lies to the northeast.",
	"korcaLocationInfo2": "The Kor\u00e7\u00eb plateau has been inhabited since prehistoric times due to its fertile soil and strategic position along ancient trade routes connecting the Adriatic and Aegean seas. The nearby archaeological site of Maliq, on the shores of a former lake, has yielded evidence of continuous habitation dating back to the Neolithic period, around 6000 BCE.",
	"korca1769ce": "After nearby Voskopojë is sacked, part of its trade moves to Korçë.",
	"korca1887ce": "The first Albanian-language school, the Mësonjëtorja, opens on 7 March.",
	"korca1891ce": "Sevasti Qiriazi founds the first Albanian school for girls.",
	"korca1916ce": "Under French protection, the Autonomous Albanian Republic of Korçë is proclaimed.",
	"korca1968ce": "The communist regime demolishes the city's Orthodox cathedral of St. George.",
	"korca1994ce": "Construction of the Resurrection Cathedral begins; it is consecrated in 2002.",

	"timelineEventIllyrianTribes": "Illyrian peoples such as the Taulantii, Ardiaei, Dardani and Enchelei take shape across the western Balkans.",
	"timelineEventEpidamnos": "Settlers from Corcyra and Corinth found Epidamnos on Taulantian land. As Dyrrachium, today's Durrës, it becomes the great port of the Adriatic coast.",
	"timelineEventBardylisDefeatsMacedon": "The Illyrian king Bardylis defeats and kills King Perdiccas III of Macedon. A year later Perdiccas's brother, Philip II, strikes back and defeats him.",
	"timelineEventAlexanderAtPelion": "At Pelion, in the south-east of today's Albania, the young Alexander the Great breaks an alliance of the Illyrian kings Cleitus and Glaukias.",
	"timelineEventGlaukiasShieldsPyrrhus": "King Glaukias of the Taulantii shelters the infant Pyrrhus of Epirus and refuses to hand him to his enemies. Years later he helps restore him to the Epirote throne.",
	"timelineEventTeutaTakesTheHelm": "After King Agron's sudden death, his widow Teuta rules the Ardiaean kingdom for his young son. Her fleets raid the Adriatic and Ionian coasts and sack Phoenice (today's Finiq), the richest city of Epirus.",
	"timelineEventTeutaDefiesRome": "Rome sends 200 ships against Queen Teuta. The First Illyrian War ends in 228 BCE with Corcyra, Epidamnos and Apollonia under Roman protection.",
	"timelineEventGentiusFalls": "In the Third Illyrian War, Rome defeats King Gentius within weeks and captures him at Scodra (Shkodër). His kingdom is broken up into districts under Roman control.",
	"timelineEventEpirusSacked": "To punish Epirus for siding with Macedon, Rome sacks 70 of its towns and sells 150,000 people into slavery. The south of today's Albania is among the lands hit.",
	"timelineEventViaEgnatia": "Rome builds the Via Egnatia, a road from Dyrrachium and Apollonia over the mountains to Thessalonica, later extended to Byzantium.",
	"timelineEventCaesarAndPompey": "In Rome's civil war, Julius Caesar besieges Pompey at Dyrrachium (Durrës) but is driven off. Weeks later he wins the war at Pharsalus in Greece.",
	"timelineEventPaulInIllyricum": "In his letter to the Romans, the apostle Paul writes that he has preached the gospel 'as far as Illyricum'.",
	"timelineEventEmpireSplits": "When Emperor Theodosius I dies, the Roman Empire is split for good. The Albanian lands fall to the eastern half, ruled from Constantinople.",
	"timelineEventAnastasiusEmperor": "Anastasius I, born in Dyrrachium (Durrës), becomes Byzantine emperor and later strengthens the walls of his home city.",
	"timelineEventButrintBaptistery": "Butrint builds a great baptistery whose mosaic floor of birds, animals and vines is among the finest early Christian mosaics in the Balkans.",
	"timelineEventCodexBeratinus": "Scribes copy the Gospels of Matthew and Mark in silver ink on purple parchment. Kept in Berat since at least 1356, the Codex Beratinus is one of the oldest Gospel books in the world.",
	"timelineEventBasilReconquers": "Emperor Basil II destroys the Bulgarian state, which had ruled much of the region, and restores Byzantine rule through the Theme of Dyrrachium.",
	"timelineEventAlbanoiNamed": "A Byzantine chronicle names 'Albanoi' among the troops of the rebel general George Maniakes, one of the first written records of the Albanians.",
	"timelineEventGreatSchism": "The churches of Rome and Constantinople split. Over time Albanian Christians divide along the same line: Catholic in the north, Orthodox in the south.",
	"timelineEventNormansAtDyrrachium": "Robert Guiscard's Normans defeat Emperor Alexios I at Dyrrachium, opening a long struggle between Normans, Venetians and Byzantines for the coast.",
	"timelineEventArbanon": "Progon, lord of Krujë, founds Arbanon, the first Albanian state of the Middle Ages. His sons Gjin and Dhimitër rule after him.",
	"timelineEventRegnumAlbaniae": "Charles of Anjou, king of Sicily, is proclaimed King of Albania (Regnum Albaniae), a Crusader-era state on the coast around Durrës.",
	"timelineEventSiegeOfBerat": "An Angevin army besieges Berat, but a Byzantine relief force captures its commander, Hugh of Sully, and the besiegers flee towards Vlorë.",
	"timelineEventVoiceInTheMountains": "A court record in Ragusa (Dubrovnik) notes: 'I heard a voice crying in the mountains in the Albanian language.' It is the earliest known mention of spoken Albanian.",
	"timelineEventALanguageOfTheirOwn": "A Dominican report to the French king notes that the Albanians have a language quite unlike Latin, but use Latin letters in their books.",
	"timelineEventAlbanianPrincipalities": "Stefan Dušan's Serbian Empire, which had conquered most Albanian lands, breaks up after his death. Families such as the Balsha, Thopia, Muzaka, Dukagjini and Kastrioti rule their own principalities.",
	"timelineEventBattleOfSavra": "At the Battle of Savra, an Ottoman army defeats and kills Balsha II, lord of Durrës. Many Albanian lords soon become Ottoman vassals.",
	"timelineEventBattleOfKosovo": "Albanian lords fight alongside Prince Lazar's Serbian army against the Ottomans on the Field of Kosovo. Both Lazar and Sultan Murad I are killed.",
	"timelineEventOttomansTakeTheSouth": "Ottoman forces capture Vlorë, Kaninë and Berat. A year later Gjirokastër falls too.",
	"timelineEventRevoltOf1432": "Albanian lords led by Gjergj Arianiti rise against Ottoman rule and win several battles before the revolt is crushed in 1436.",
	"timelineEventSkanderbegReturns": "Gjergj Kastrioti, an Ottoman commander known as Skanderbeg, deserts at the Battle of Niš, takes Krujë on 28 November and raises his red flag with the black double-headed eagle.",
	"timelineEventLeagueOfLezhe": "Albanian princes unite under Skanderbeg in the League of Lezhë. Months later they win their first great victory, at Torvioll.",
	"timelineEventFirstSiegeOfKruja": "Sultan Murad II besieges Krujë for months with a huge army, but the garrison holds out and he withdraws.",
	"timelineEventBattleOfAlbulena": "Skanderbeg routs a large Ottoman army at Albulena (Ujëbardha), one of his most celebrated victories.",
	"timelineEventBaptismalFormula": "Archbishop Pal Engjëlli writes a baptismal formula in Albanian into a Latin letter: 'Un'te paghesont' pr'emenit t'Atit e t'Birit e t'Spertit Senit.' It is the oldest known written Albanian sentence.",
	"timelineEventMehmedsSieges": "Sultan Mehmed II, conqueror of Constantinople, besieges Krujë twice and builds the fortress of Elbasan to cut Skanderbeg's lands in two. Krujë still holds.",
	"timelineEventSkanderbegDies": "Skanderbeg dies of fever in Lezhë on 17 January. Without him, the resistance slowly gives way.",
	"timelineEventArbereshMigration": "As the Ottomans advance, thousands of Albanians cross to southern Italy and Sicily. Their descendants, the Arbëreshë, still speak Albanian in dozens of villages today.",
	"timelineEventShkoderFalls": "After a long siege, Venice hands Shkodër to the Ottomans. Almost all of Albania is now under Ottoman rule; Durrës follows in 1501.",
	"timelineEventMeshari": "Gjon Buzuku, a Catholic priest, completes the Meshari (Missal), the oldest surviving book in Albanian. A single copy survives, in the Vatican Library.",
	"timelineEventBardhiDictionary": "Frang Bardhi publishes a Latin–Albanian dictionary in Rome, the first dictionary of the Albanian language.",
	"timelineEventIslamization": "Over the 17th century most Albanians convert to Islam, often to escape heavy taxes and to rise in Ottoman service. Catholic and Orthodox communities endure.",
	"timelineEventCuneusProphetarum": "Archbishop Pjetër Bogdani publishes Cuneus Prophetarum (The Band of the Prophets) in Padua, the first major original work of Albanian prose.",
	"timelineEventVoskopojePress": "Voskopojë, a rich trading town near Korçë, opens one of the first printing presses in the Ottoman Balkans. In 1744 it founds the New Academy.",
	"timelineEventBushatiPashalik": "Mehmed Pasha Bushati makes Shkodër the seat of a near-independent pashalik that his family rules until 1831.",
	"timelineEventAliPashaRises": "Ali Pasha becomes ruler of Ioannina and builds a near-independent state across southern Albania and mainland Greece.",
	"timelineEventAliPashaFalls": "After a long siege at Ioannina, the Sultan's agents kill the elderly Ali Pasha and send his head to Constantinople.",
	"timelineEventMassacreOfTheBeys": "The Ottoman grand vizier invites Albanian beys to Manastir with a promise of amnesty and has some 500 of them killed, breaking the power of the old lords.",
	"timelineEventVeqilharxhiPrimer": "Naum Veqilharxhi publishes an Albanian spelling book in an alphabet of his own invention, one of the first works of the National Awakening.",
	"timelineEventLeagueOfPrizren": "As the Great Powers prepare to hand Albanian-inhabited lands to Serbia and Montenegro, Albanian leaders meet in Prizren to defend them. It is the first organised national movement; Ottoman troops crush it in 1881.",
	"timelineEventIstanbulAlphabet": "Sami Frashëri and fellow patriots found a society in Istanbul to print books in Albanian and publish an alphabet for the language.",
	"timelineEventHerdsAndFields": "Naim Frashëri publishes Bagëti e Bujqësi (Herds and Fields), a hymn to the Albanian countryside that becomes the best-loved poem of the Awakening.",
	"timelineEventFirstAlbanianSchool": "On 7 March, the Mësonjëtorja opens in Korçë, the first school to teach in Albanian.",
	"timelineEventSchoolForGirls": "Sevasti Qiriazi founds the first Albanian school for girls in Korçë, where girls learn to read and write in their own language.",
	"timelineEventSamiManifesto": "Sami Frashëri publishes Albania: What It Was, What It Is and What It Will Be, a blueprint for a free Albanian state.",
	"timelineEventFirstAlbanianLiturgy": "In Boston, Fan Noli celebrates the first Orthodox liturgy in the Albanian language, the start of an Albanian Orthodox church.",
	"timelineEventCongressOfManastir": "The Congress of Manastir adopts the Latin-based Albanian alphabet that is still used today.",
	"timelineEventFlagAtDeciq": "During the highland uprising of 1911, Ded Gjo Luli's fighters win at Deçiq and raise the Albanian flag, for the first time since the 15th century.",
	"timelineEventIndependence": "On 28 November, Ismail Qemali raises the flag in Vlorë and declares Albania independent of the Ottoman Empire.",
	"timelineEventLondonBorders": "The Great Powers recognise an independent Albania but leave Kosovo and other Albanian-inhabited lands outside its borders.",
	"timelineEventPrinceWied": "Prince Wilhelm of Wied arrives to rule the new Principality of Albania. Within six months, revolt and World War I drive him out.",
	"timelineEventRepublicOfKorca": "Under French military protection, Korçë becomes an autonomous Albanian republic with its own schools, stamps and flag.",
	"timelineEventCongressOfLushnje": "The Congress of Lushnjë rejects plans to divide Albania among its neighbours and makes Tirana the capital.",
	"timelineEventVloraWar": "Albanian volunteers besiege the Italian army in Vlorë, and by September Italy withdraws from mainland Albania. In December, Albania joins the League of Nations.",
	"timelineEventOrthodoxAutocephaly": "The Congress of Berat declares the Albanian Orthodox Church autocephalous, independent of the Patriarchate of Constantinople.",
	"timelineEventJuneRevolution": "A revolt brings Bishop Fan Noli to power with a programme of reforms. Six months later Ahmet Zogu returns with foreign backing and Noli flees.",
	"timelineEventKingZog": "President Ahmet Zogu proclaims himself Zog I, King of the Albanians.",
	"timelineEventBektashiCentre": "Banned in Turkey, the Bektashi Sufi order decides at a congress in Korçë to move its world headquarters to Tirana, where it remains today.",
	"timelineEventKanunPublished": "Shtjefën Gjeçovi's collection of the Kanun of Lekë Dukagjini, the customary law of the northern highlands, is published in full.",
	"timelineEventItalianInvasion": "On 7 April, Mussolini's troops land at Durrës and other ports. King Zog flees, and Albania is joined to the Italian crown.",
	"timelineEventKokalariBook": "Musine Kokalari publishes As My Old Mother Tells Me, stories in the dialect of Gjirokastër. She is the first Albanian woman to publish prose.",
	"timelineEventWarWithGreece": "Italy attacks Greece from Albanian soil. The Greek counter-attack pushes deep into the south, taking Korçë in November and Gjirokastër in December.",
	"timelineEventResistanceOrganises": "At the Peza Conference, communists and nationalists form the National Liberation Movement. The rival nationalist Balli Kombëtar is founded later that year.",
	"timelineEventGermanOccupation": "Italy surrenders in September and German troops occupy Albania. The rival resistance groups now fight each other as well as the occupiers.",
	"timelineEventBesaJews": "Albanian families, Muslim and Christian, hide Jewish refugees from the occupiers, honouring besa, a sacred promise. Albania ends the war with more Jews than it had before.",
	"timelineEventGovernmentAtBerat": "Meeting in Berat in October, the communist-led resistance council forms a provisional government with Enver Hoxha as prime minister.",
	"timelineEventLiberation": "On 29 November the last German troops leave. The communist-led partisans take power, and Enver Hoxha begins four decades of one-party rule.",
	"timelineEventPeoplesRepublic": "Albania is proclaimed a People's Republic. Opponents are tried and imprisoned, among them the writer Musine Kokalari.",
	"timelineEventCorfuChannel": "Mines in the Corfu Channel badly damage two British destroyers and kill 44 sailors. The dispute becomes the first case judged by the International Court of Justice.",
	"timelineEventBreakWithYugoslavia": "When Tito breaks with Stalin, Hoxha cuts ties with Yugoslavia and turns to the Soviet Union.",
	"timelineEventBreakWithMoscow": "Albania sides with China against Khrushchev. The Soviet Union breaks off relations and pulls its forces out of the naval base at Vlorë.",
	"timelineEventAtheistState": "The regime closes or destroys mosques and churches and declares Albania the world's first atheist state.",
	"timelineEventBunkers": "Fearing invasion, the regime covers the country with concrete bunkers, some 170,000 of them, built from the late 1960s to the mid-1980s.",
	"timelineEventChronicleInStone": "Ismail Kadare publishes Chronicle in Stone, which shows his native Gjirokastër during World War II through the eyes of a boy.",
	"timelineEventOrthographicCongress": "The Congress of Orthography fixes a single standard for written Albanian, based mainly on the southern Tosk dialect.",
	"timelineEventBreakWithChina": "China ends its aid, and Albania breaks with its last ally, relying only on itself.",
	"timelineEventMotherTeresaNobel": "Mother Teresa, born Anjezë Gonxhe Bojaxhiu to an Albanian family in Skopje, receives the Nobel Peace Prize for her work among the poor of Calcutta.",
	"timelineEventHoxhaDies": "Enver Hoxha dies on 11 April. His successor, Ramiz Alia, loosens controls only slowly.",
	"timelineEventRegimeCracks": "In July, thousands of Albanians rush into foreign embassies to leave the country. In December, student protests in Tirana force the regime to allow opposition parties.",
	"timelineEventFaithReturns": "On 4 November, the priest Simon Jubani says the first public Mass in decades, in a cemetery in Shkodër. Within months, mosques and churches reopen across the country.",
	"timelineEventFirstFreeElections": "In March, Albania holds its first multi-party elections in decades. The former communists win, but a year later the Democratic Party takes power.",
	"timelineEventVloraShip": "On 8 August the freighter Vlora reaches Bari carrying an estimated 20,000 Albanians, the defining image of a mass exodus.",
	"timelineEventPyramidCrisis": "The collapse of pyramid investment schemes wipes out the savings of much of the country and sets off armed unrest in which more than 2,000 people die.",
	"timelineEventKosovoRefugees": "During the Kosovo war, Albania takes in some 450,000 refugees, most of them in private homes.",
	"timelineEventIsoPolyphony": "UNESCO proclaims Albanian folk iso-polyphony, the many-voiced singing of the south, a masterpiece of humanity's oral and intangible heritage.",
	"timelineEventKadareBooker": "Ismail Kadare wins the first Man Booker International Prize for his body of work.",
	"timelineEventNatoMembership": "Albania becomes a member of NATO on 1 April and applies to join the European Union weeks later.",
	"timelineEventEuCandidate": "Albania becomes an official candidate for membership of the European Union.",
	"timelineEventDurresEarthquake": "A magnitude 6.4 earthquake strikes near Durrës on 26 November, killing 51 people and leaving thousands homeless.",
	"timelineEventEuTalks": "Albania formally opens accession negotiations with the European Union on 19 July.",
